analysis:
Reviewed both primary units. The PR adds the Darkbloom watchdog CI workflow (.github/workflows/darkbloom-watchdog.yml, not visible in the replayed source), a changelog entry (changelog.d/ci-darkbloom-watchdog-on-master.md lines 1-3), and pins in scripts/test-darkbloom-route.py. No provider, coordinator, attestation, key, cache or fan-helper code is changed. The threat model's TB-002 to TB-013, A-001 to A-019 and T-041 cover the provider runtime, not CI scheduling, so no threat IDs apply.

The visible test changes tighten guardrails. test_watchdog_rescues_only_this_repositorys_routed_runs (head lines 591-617) pins these properties:
- triggers are schedule and workflow_dispatch only;
- top-level permissions are {};
- the job runs on ubuntu-24.04 with only actions:write and checks:write;
- there is no uses, secrets, checkout, id-token or contents;
- API calls are scoped to $REPOSITORY;
- it acts only on runs with a queued self-hosted darkbloom-* job.

It also asserts that UNROUTED release, signing and credentialed workflows never carry darkbloom or self-hosted runners (lines 609-612). The label-ban exemption (lines 579-580) is narrow, an exact filename match. The test does not weaken any existing assertion.

The workflow body is absent from the replayed evidence. Only the test assertions and an earlier unit analysis describe it. I could not independently verify the shell/jq handling, the marker check-run trust gating, or the rescue bounds. Cancel and re-run authority is limited to this repository's Actions and cannot reach provider secrets, so I see no concrete security regression. The residual question is CI-only and sits outside the provider threat-model assets: confirm that the workflow file matches what the tests assert, especially injection safety and the bounded rescue count.
